<Styles xmlns="https://github.com/avaloniaui"
        xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
        xmlns:luna="clr-namespace:EmuSen.LunaP.Controls">

    <!-- Transparent rather than Fluent's own chrome colour, so the strip is the
         window's LunaSurface and a theme that repaints the window repaints the
         menu bar with it. Fluent's Menu paints #2C2C2C on dark, which against
         LunaSurface's #1E1E1E reads as a lighter bar somebody forgot to style.

         `Menu.luna-menu-bar` AND NOT `luna|MenuBar`, which is the whole of §30
         and is not a matter of taste. MenuBar pins StyleKeyOverride to
         typeof(Menu) so the Fluent template reaches it at all - without that the
         bar draws no menus. But Avalonia matches a type selector against the
         STYLE KEY rather than the runtime type, so `luna|MenuBar` asks for a
         control whose style key is MenuBar, and by construction there is never
         one. The selector this file shipped with from §26 to §29 matched nothing
         and said so to nobody; both setters below were dead for four releases.

         The class is what a selector can still name. MenuBar adds it to itself
         in its constructor and publishes it as MenuBar.StyleClass, so this
         string and the one the CSS vocabulary uses are the same string.

         WHY IT WENT UNNOTICED, worth keeping because it is the reason no test
         caught it: Avalonia paints a Menu transparent anyway, so the intent here
         and the appearance on screen agreed by luck. Padding is the half that
         did not - it read 0,0,0,0 at priority Unset, meaning nothing anywhere
         set it. ShellTests asserts the padding for exactly that reason. -->
    <Style Selector="Menu.luna-menu-bar">
        <Setter Property="Background" Value="Transparent" />
        <Setter Property="Padding" Value="2,0" />
    </Style>

</Styles>
